# defense-messages store snapshot
# defense-type files at time of writing: 9
# defense-types: block,counter-defy,counter-melee,counter-quell,counter-ranged,defy,dodge,parry,quell
# dimensions: defense-type x band(weak/normal/heavy) -- all 3 roles (todefender/toattacker/toroom)
# come from ONE coordinated RenderDefenseMessage call (same index across the triad).
# RenderDefenseMessage has no picker param (util.Rand only) -- pinned via indexOverride=0,
# the fresh-SequencePicker-first-pick equivalent for this seam.

# ALSO covers the MELEE seam (internal/combat sendDefenseMessages), which does its own
# zScore banding via items.GetDefenseMessage and used to pick each of the 3 roles with an
# INDEPENDENT MessageOptions.Get() call -- three unrelated random indices describing three
# different events. That bug shipped invisibly because this snapshot only ever exercised
# RenderDefenseMessage, never GetDefenseMessage. The melee| rows below freeze
# GetDefenseMessage(...).RenderTriad(...) (a fresh SequencePicker per tuple, so index 0 every
# time) so a regression back to three independent picks shows up here again.

block|weak|todefender => <ansi fg="defense-good">You barely block <ansi fg="user">Attacker</ansi>'s attack!</ansi>
block|weak|toattacker => <ansi fg="attack-bad"><ansi fg="mob">Defender</ansi> barely blocks your attack!</ansi>
block|weak|toroom => <ansi fg="combat"><ansi fg="mob">Defender</ansi> barely blocks <ansi fg="user">Attacker</ansi>'s attack.</ansi>
block|normal|todefender => <ansi fg="defense-good">You block <ansi fg="user">Attacker</ansi>'s attack!</ansi>
block|normal|toattacker => <ansi fg="attack-bad"><ansi fg="mob">Defender</ansi> blocks your attack!</ansi>
block|normal|toroom => <ansi fg="combat"><ansi fg="mob">Defender</ansi> blocks <ansi fg="user">Attacker</ansi>'s attack.</ansi>
